# FuturAmerican

**FuturAmerican** (also **FuturAmerican Kind**) is a fictional constructed variety of English, designed as a blend of dialect features from American communities. It is part of the Futurologism-0 project. [C-0]

| | |
|---|---|
| **Type** | Constructed variety of English (fictional) |
| **Structure** | Shared core plus eight switchable layers |
| **Created** | 2026 (worldbuilding) |

## Overview
FuturAmerican keeps a shared English core and lets speakers move between layers, rather than merging every dialect into one flattened standard. Switching between layers is expected, not an error.

## Layers (sub-varieties)
Each layer draws only on documented linguistic features of its source communities.

- **Latino Layer:** mid-sentence Spanish-English code-switching, syllable-timed rhythm.
- **Black Layer:** habitual *be*, copula deletion, rich oral-rhetoric traditions.
- **Asian Layer:** heritage-language switching and loanwords from many source languages.
- **Heartland Layer:** regional features like double modals (*might could*) and Yiddish-origin vocabulary already common in English.
- **Indigenous Layer:** Native English prosody and pacing, plus Indigenous-language loanwords and place names.
- **MENA Layer:** Arabic, Persian, and other loanwords, plus code-switching.
- **Pacific Layer:** Hawaiʻi Creole features such as the past marker *wen*, plus Pacific loanwords.
- **Bridge Layer:** the mixed-heritage layer, built for fluid movement between all the others.

## Code-switching
Layers connect pairwise, so any layer can switch to any other, with the core as the fallback.

## Criticism
Linguists would warn that "blending dialects" risks caricature, and that real dialects are communities' living speech, not raw material. *[citation needed]*

